PUT belongs in the Edit/Save function, just like PATCH.

We haven’t used it because our GearDesk API accepts PATCH for updates and currently does not support PUT.
Method	Typical purpose
POST	Create a record
PUT	Replace a record’s representation with the supplied complete data
PATCH	Update selected fields


PUT’s replacement behavior is why you generally send the complete set of fields required by that API. MDN

For an API that documents PUT, your Edit handler could contain:
// updateUrl must be the endpoint documented by that API.
const response = await fetch(updateUrl, {
  method: "PUT",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    Authorization: "Bearer " + token.value
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    name: editName.value.trim(),
    category_id: Number(editCategory.value),
    daily_rate: Number(editRate.value),
    available_units: Number(editUnits.value)
  })
});

The modal’s HTML and CSS can stay the same. The request method, endpoint, and required payload follow the backend documentation.

For the assessment: check whether Update specifies PUT, PATCH, or even POST, then implement that exact contract.















